WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:09.330
&gt;&gt; Welcome Susan.

00:00:09.331 --> 00:00:10.925
It's really fabulous to have you

00:00:10.926 --> 00:00:12.735
here with us at
Microsoft Research.

00:00:12.736 --> 00:00:15.410
Of course, Microsoft is

00:00:15.411 --> 00:00:18.060
almost a home for
you in some ways.

00:00:18.061 --> 00:00:20.555
&gt;&gt; That's right. It's
great to be here.

00:00:20.556 --> 00:00:22.590
&gt;&gt;Yeah. You were
our Chief Economist,

00:00:22.591 --> 00:00:23.865
what years was that?

00:00:23.866 --> 00:00:27.905
&gt;&gt; Well, so, I
started in 2007 and I

00:00:27.906 --> 00:00:32.240
think they endowed me with
that fancy title in early-

00:00:32.241 --> 00:00:33.280
&gt;&gt; It's a great title.

00:00:33.281 --> 00:00:36.770
&gt;&gt; -2008 and really there
hadn't been a Chief Economist.

00:00:36.771 --> 00:00:41.060
So, they decided to create one.

00:00:41.061 --> 00:00:42.970
Now, I describe it as

00:00:42.971 --> 00:00:44.800
Consulting Chief Economist
because I had

00:00:44.801 --> 00:00:48.995
a mix of full-time
as a VR for a while,

00:00:48.996 --> 00:00:51.395
and then part-time.

00:00:51.396 --> 00:00:54.775
I did that for about seven years.

00:00:54.776 --> 00:00:56.970
&gt;&gt; Just so folks out
there know what a VR is,

00:00:56.971 --> 00:00:58.640
Visiting Researcher ship.
It used to be VR.

00:00:58.641 --> 00:01:00.230
&gt;&gt; Exactly. So,
Microsoft Research has

00:01:00.231 --> 00:01:02.030
this great program where without

00:01:02.031 --> 00:01:04.255
resigning in your
university position,

00:01:04.256 --> 00:01:07.015
you can basically be
full-time at Microsoft.

00:01:07.016 --> 00:01:08.990
So, I had a home in
Microsoft Research,

00:01:08.991 --> 00:01:11.210
but was also doing
spending a lot of

00:01:11.211 --> 00:01:13.615
time with the business as well.

00:01:13.616 --> 00:01:15.180
&gt;&gt; Jumping way back in time,

00:01:15.181 --> 00:01:20.050
not that far back to
your early college years.

00:01:20.051 --> 00:01:22.140
I'm just curious because
a lot of the folks

00:01:22.141 --> 00:01:25.230
that come in and watch
these Fireside Chats are

00:01:25.231 --> 00:01:29.090
very interested in careers
and mentorship and folks who

00:01:29.091 --> 00:01:33.405
inspired folks to do what
they now do in the world.

00:01:33.406 --> 00:01:37.260
I'm curious about your own
education and experience.

00:01:37.261 --> 00:01:38.750
You are at Duke and

00:01:38.751 --> 00:01:42.590
it seemed you were so excited
about so many things.

00:01:42.591 --> 00:01:43.640
You had multiple majors.

00:01:43.641 --> 00:01:45.620
So, what went on in
your early days of college,

00:01:45.621 --> 00:01:46.670
in terms of your thinking about

00:01:46.671 --> 00:01:48.670
career and topics of interest?

00:01:48.671 --> 00:01:50.720
&gt;&gt; Sure. So, I
actually started in

00:01:50.721 --> 00:01:52.850
computer science and I had

00:01:52.851 --> 00:01:54.470
gotten a little bit
of exposure to

00:01:54.471 --> 00:01:57.370
computer science as a summer job,

00:01:57.371 --> 00:01:58.870
selling computers
to the government,

00:01:58.871 --> 00:02:00.380
the summer before college.

00:02:00.381 --> 00:02:02.030
So, I decided I wanted to

00:02:02.031 --> 00:02:03.740
do computer science
and I just took

00:02:03.741 --> 00:02:05.360
a few economics classes because I

00:02:05.361 --> 00:02:07.170
thought maybe I would
go to a startup.

00:02:07.171 --> 00:02:08.585
Of course going to a startup

00:02:08.586 --> 00:02:10.320
in the late 80's wasn't really

00:02:10.321 --> 00:02:14.520
the trendy thing to do but
it seemed intriguing to me.

00:02:14.521 --> 00:02:17.000
Then, I learned about

00:02:17.001 --> 00:02:20.030
economics and I actually
had a professor in

00:02:20.031 --> 00:02:21.830
economics who picked me out to be

00:02:21.831 --> 00:02:23.480
his research assistant
and I actually

00:02:23.481 --> 00:02:25.580
started administering
his workstation.

00:02:25.581 --> 00:02:26.770
He said, "Oh, I'd love it,

00:02:26.771 --> 00:02:28.625
if you got a PhD in economics."

00:02:28.626 --> 00:02:30.730
I said. "Well, I don't
really think I'm going to-"

00:02:30.731 --> 00:02:31.865
&gt;&gt;This is as undergraduate.

00:02:31.866 --> 00:02:32.900
&gt;&gt; As an undergraduate. Yeah.

00:02:32.901 --> 00:02:34.095
&gt;&gt; Administering his workstation

00:02:34.096 --> 00:02:35.680
and it happened as mentioned his-

00:02:35.681 --> 00:02:36.070
&gt;&gt; Well-

00:02:36.071 --> 00:02:37.235
&gt;&gt; Impassing.

00:02:37.236 --> 00:02:41.000
&gt;&gt; He was really looking to
mentor students to go on

00:02:41.001 --> 00:02:42.320
to get PhDs' and he'd had

00:02:42.321 --> 00:02:44.390
a number of wonderful
undergraduates.

00:02:44.391 --> 00:02:46.365
Actually, it's a nice thing
about being at

00:02:46.366 --> 00:02:48.440
a program that has
a good PhD program,

00:02:48.441 --> 00:02:50.410
but not a great PhD program
because at Duke,

00:02:50.411 --> 00:02:52.190
the undergraduates
were a little stronger

00:02:52.191 --> 00:02:54.240
on average than the PhD students.

00:02:54.241 --> 00:02:55.100
So, he had been

00:02:55.101 --> 00:02:56.870
recruiting undergraduate
research assistant.

00:02:56.871 --> 00:02:58.295
&gt;&gt; That's interesting.
What was his name?

00:02:58.296 --> 00:02:59.360
&gt;&gt; Bob Marshall.

00:02:59.361 --> 00:03:01.340
&gt;&gt; So, did you view him as

00:03:01.341 --> 00:03:02.870
an inspiring force in your life

00:03:02.871 --> 00:03:04.490
at that point or bridging to?

00:03:04.491 --> 00:03:06.410
&gt;&gt; - at first I just
liked to administering

00:03:06.411 --> 00:03:08.690
a Unix workstation better
than washing test tubes,

00:03:08.691 --> 00:03:10.880
which was my next best
work study alternative

00:03:10.881 --> 00:03:12.560
to pay for my school.

00:03:12.561 --> 00:03:14.090
But then, over time, I

00:03:14.091 --> 00:03:16.760
actually got very
excited about economics.

00:03:16.761 --> 00:03:18.710
At the time, computer science

00:03:18.711 --> 00:03:22.390
was maybe a little bit more
theoretical and abstract.

00:03:22.391 --> 00:03:24.335
So I loved it. But I also,

00:03:24.336 --> 00:03:26.015
it was a little bit harder to see

00:03:26.016 --> 00:03:28.700
the mapping beyond programming,

00:03:28.701 --> 00:03:29.900
the mapping from really

00:03:29.901 --> 00:03:31.980
the research side
of computer science

00:03:31.981 --> 00:03:33.990
to things that were

00:03:33.991 --> 00:03:35.860
more practical and
it sounds crazy now,

00:03:35.861 --> 00:03:37.550
because of course,
computer science

00:03:37.551 --> 00:03:38.760
is applied everywhere.

00:03:38.761 --> 00:03:42.465
But in economics, I felt
a little bit more of

00:03:42.466 --> 00:03:43.910
a natural way to see

00:03:43.911 --> 00:03:46.680
how the research would
have changed the world.

00:03:46.681 --> 00:03:49.280
So, I liked the impact
of economics and I

00:03:49.281 --> 00:03:50.600
actually worked on
a bunch of policy

00:03:50.601 --> 00:03:51.915
problems with this professor.

00:03:51.916 --> 00:03:53.880
He really showed me how research,

00:03:53.881 --> 00:03:55.040
he end up testifying before

00:03:55.041 --> 00:03:56.240
Congress on something I worked

00:03:56.241 --> 00:03:58.650
with him on and so I
got excited about that.

00:03:58.651 --> 00:04:00.560
But I never lost my love
of computer science,

00:04:00.561 --> 00:04:02.090
so I finished
the computer science major

00:04:02.091 --> 00:04:03.745
and the economics major.

00:04:03.746 --> 00:04:06.540
The math major came along
the way because I had taken

00:04:06.541 --> 00:04:09.020
a pre-theoretical computer
science approach and I

00:04:09.021 --> 00:04:10.340
also needed a good math to get

00:04:10.341 --> 00:04:12.155
into the best PhD programs.

00:04:12.156 --> 00:04:14.840
But then, I went into

00:04:14.841 --> 00:04:18.964
the economic direction
and studied auctions,

00:04:18.965 --> 00:04:23.090
but I never lost that love
of computer science.

00:04:23.091 --> 00:04:25.290
So, it was then,

00:04:25.291 --> 00:04:28.020
as I got further in my career,

00:04:28.021 --> 00:04:29.780
that these things came back

00:04:29.781 --> 00:04:31.790
together in really
interesting ways.

00:04:31.791 --> 00:04:34.670
&gt;&gt; To shifting to topical,

00:04:34.671 --> 00:04:38.720
it seems there's a thread of
fascination that you've had

00:04:38.721 --> 00:04:41.960
with action or decisions under

00:04:41.961 --> 00:04:45.755
uncertainty and grappling with
uncertainty in the world,

00:04:45.756 --> 00:04:48.140
and looking at economics through

00:04:48.141 --> 00:04:50.465
that lens of action
decision making.

00:04:50.466 --> 00:04:52.410
You've any comments
about this idea,

00:04:52.411 --> 00:04:54.050
of this passion or interest

00:04:54.051 --> 00:04:57.035
in uncertainty, in probability?

00:04:57.036 --> 00:05:01.300
&gt;&gt; I actually, I would
say that generally

00:05:01.301 --> 00:05:03.160
my interest in theoretical issues

00:05:03.161 --> 00:05:05.085
has always been as
a means to an end.

00:05:05.086 --> 00:05:07.240
So, early in my career,

00:05:07.241 --> 00:05:09.100
I was interested in
studying problems about

00:05:09.101 --> 00:05:11.320
auctions and mechanism
design and then

00:05:11.321 --> 00:05:13.105
there were some technical issues

00:05:13.106 --> 00:05:14.980
that needed to be solved to

00:05:14.981 --> 00:05:18.950
really make that
theory beautiful.

00:05:18.951 --> 00:05:20.320
So, in some ways,

00:05:20.321 --> 00:05:22.000
it was a little bit
unnatural for me because

00:05:22.001 --> 00:05:24.055
I got more theoretical and

00:05:24.056 --> 00:05:28.010
I became very well known
for very theoretical work.

00:05:28.011 --> 00:05:30.800
I loved doing it and
it was beautiful,

00:05:30.801 --> 00:05:32.965
but actually it wasn't
my main motivation.

00:05:32.966 --> 00:05:34.330
My main motivation was to

00:05:34.331 --> 00:05:37.129
solve these slightly
more applied problems,

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:38.440
as a means to an end.

00:05:38.441 --> 00:05:40.960
So, I think the class of
problems I was interested in

00:05:40.961 --> 00:05:46.940
were games under uncertainty,
auctions and mechanisms,

00:05:46.941 --> 00:05:48.610
and really designing markets,

00:05:48.611 --> 00:05:51.910
trying to figure out
how you can design

00:05:51.911 --> 00:05:54.310
institutions or rules
of games to make

00:05:54.311 --> 00:05:56.930
them work better and
work more efficiently.

00:05:56.931 --> 00:05:59.660
&gt;&gt; But it seem that
the real world but

00:05:59.661 --> 00:06:01.520
the uncertainty as
the critical factor

00:06:01.521 --> 00:06:03.605
and tell us the reason
about in some ways.

00:06:03.606 --> 00:06:07.115
&gt;&gt; In theory of auctions,

00:06:07.116 --> 00:06:10.994
the ideas that
the mechanism designer,

00:06:10.995 --> 00:06:13.965
the auctioneer doesn't have

00:06:13.966 --> 00:06:16.120
all the information
about how valuable

00:06:16.121 --> 00:06:18.890
an object is to the participants.

00:06:18.891 --> 00:06:22.505
So part of the role
of the auction is to

00:06:22.506 --> 00:06:24.900
get that information elicited and

00:06:24.901 --> 00:06:27.225
then use it to make
efficient decisions.

00:06:27.226 --> 00:06:30.150
But the nature of the game can

00:06:30.151 --> 00:06:33.510
lead to efficiency or
inefficiency and how

00:06:33.511 --> 00:06:37.000
the bidders respond to
that uncertainty as well as how

00:06:37.001 --> 00:06:39.745
the auction design elicits

00:06:39.746 --> 00:06:41.425
behavior can make a difference.

00:06:41.426 --> 00:06:43.290
So just one application of that,

00:06:43.291 --> 00:06:45.300
we looked at for example

00:06:45.301 --> 00:06:47.435
the difference between,
for timber auctions,

00:06:47.436 --> 00:06:48.910
the difference between having

00:06:48.911 --> 00:06:50.460
an open ascending
auction where people

00:06:50.461 --> 00:06:52.565
raised their hands and till

00:06:52.566 --> 00:06:54.730
the auction ends so
that everybody drops

00:06:54.731 --> 00:06:57.210
out versus the first price
seal bid auction where you

00:06:57.211 --> 00:06:59.135
send in sealed bids and then

00:06:59.136 --> 00:07:02.185
the winner is the highest bidder
and they pay their bid.

00:07:02.186 --> 00:07:04.290
So, under some conditions there's

00:07:04.291 --> 00:07:05.580
a beautiful theory that says that

00:07:05.581 --> 00:07:07.390
those are actually equivalent.

00:07:07.391 --> 00:07:09.550
But then under
real world conditions,

00:07:09.551 --> 00:07:10.760
there's a bunch of
reasons they're not

00:07:10.761 --> 00:07:12.180
equivalent and in particular,

00:07:12.181 --> 00:07:15.895
the ones where you have
sealed bids are inefficient.

00:07:15.896 --> 00:07:18.510
That is a weaker bidder can win.

00:07:18.511 --> 00:07:20.800
It's basically these people
shade their beards and

00:07:20.801 --> 00:07:22.850
weaker bidders are
more aggressive bidders and they

00:07:22.851 --> 00:07:24.720
can outbid someone
who thinks they're

00:07:24.721 --> 00:07:25.890
likely to win and doesn't think

00:07:25.891 --> 00:07:27.295
they need to be so aggressive.

00:07:27.296 --> 00:07:29.395
So that seems like that's bad

00:07:29.396 --> 00:07:31.375
and so you shouldn't
do that but it has

00:07:31.376 --> 00:07:33.000
this extra benefit in that it

00:07:33.001 --> 00:07:35.555
induces more participation
of weak bidders.

00:07:35.556 --> 00:07:37.680
So the bidder's understanding

00:07:37.681 --> 00:07:39.245
that they have
a chance to sneak in,

00:07:39.246 --> 00:07:41.415
and when and efficiently are
more likely to participate,

00:07:41.416 --> 00:07:43.670
and that in turn can fight

00:07:43.671 --> 00:07:47.975
collusion and lead to
more competitive auctions.

00:07:47.976 --> 00:07:50.140
So I did a lot of
research about that

00:07:50.141 --> 00:07:52.230
theoretically and
also working with

00:07:52.231 --> 00:07:54.560
data from real timber
auctions to illustrate

00:07:54.561 --> 00:07:57.075
the magnitudes of
those effects and

00:07:57.076 --> 00:07:59.390
those types of themes
that the rules of

00:07:59.391 --> 00:08:02.235
the game have
these direct consequences,

00:08:02.236 --> 00:08:06.570
but also this broader
implications for how competitive

00:08:06.571 --> 00:08:08.985
a market is and who even
participates in the market

00:08:08.986 --> 00:08:11.750
is a theme that then
I brought later to,

00:08:11.751 --> 00:08:13.250
say, the advertising options

00:08:13.251 --> 00:08:15.085
that the search engine
in Microsoft.

00:08:15.086 --> 00:08:18.170
&gt;&gt; That was really exciting to

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:23.750
identify one or more
pathologies and occur in

00:08:23.751 --> 00:08:26.700
understanding and or
current implemented

00:08:26.701 --> 00:08:30.130
mechanism that lead
to things like

00:08:30.131 --> 00:08:33.730
collusion having big effects and

00:08:33.731 --> 00:08:35.150
coming up with a new

00:08:35.151 --> 00:08:37.430
clean beautiful theory
that addresses

00:08:37.431 --> 00:08:41.345
some open world issues
that then give

00:08:41.346 --> 00:08:45.270
you a new more bullet
proof mechanism.

00:08:45.271 --> 00:08:48.245
Must be a feel good to
have it take effect.

00:08:48.246 --> 00:08:51.105
&gt;&gt; Yes. I think
one thing that I think

00:08:51.106 --> 00:08:54.335
economics really
trains you well for

00:08:54.336 --> 00:08:57.615
is trying to get a model with
just the right amount of

00:08:57.616 --> 00:08:59.710
abstraction, that it can

00:08:59.711 --> 00:09:02.845
capture the important
forces in the world.

00:09:02.846 --> 00:09:04.890
Then, the model is also specific

00:09:04.891 --> 00:09:08.180
enough that it makes
sharp predictions

00:09:08.181 --> 00:09:11.140
and it can be hopefully
taken the data

00:09:11.141 --> 00:09:14.095
and some economic models
really flunk that.

00:09:14.096 --> 00:09:15.670
They're too complicated,
they are too

00:09:15.671 --> 00:09:17.675
unrealistic and you just
don't fit the data.

00:09:17.676 --> 00:09:20.280
But in mechanism design
and auctions has been

00:09:20.281 --> 00:09:23.190
a real success of being able

00:09:23.191 --> 00:09:25.770
to build the theory
that's actually

00:09:25.771 --> 00:09:27.835
useful and then calibrate

00:09:27.836 --> 00:09:29.810
it with data and
make predictions.

00:09:29.811 --> 00:09:32.450
So one of the things that
we did in the work on

00:09:32.451 --> 00:09:33.780
the two different
types of auctions

00:09:33.781 --> 00:09:35.260
is we estimated the model,

00:09:35.261 --> 00:09:36.835
trained the model using

00:09:36.836 --> 00:09:39.135
just the data from
the sealed bid auctions,

00:09:39.136 --> 00:09:41.710
the the first price
still bit auctions.

00:09:41.711 --> 00:09:43.970
So we calibrated those
models and we figured

00:09:43.971 --> 00:09:46.245
out on average what
bidder preferences were,

00:09:46.246 --> 00:09:49.760
and then we predicted not
just how they would bid,

00:09:49.761 --> 00:09:51.830
but also how many would
even participate,

00:09:51.831 --> 00:09:54.660
in the open ascending auction,
without using any data.

00:09:54.661 --> 00:09:56.175
So it's a completely
different game

00:09:56.176 --> 00:09:57.960
and actually theory
would just that

00:09:57.961 --> 00:09:59.950
actually half as
many small bidders

00:09:59.951 --> 00:10:01.875
should show up at
the open ascending auction.

00:10:01.876 --> 00:10:03.715
So it's a really different game.

00:10:03.716 --> 00:10:06.140
But using just the data
from the seal bit auctions,

00:10:06.141 --> 00:10:07.890
we could actually do
the counterfactual prediction

00:10:07.891 --> 00:10:09.290
of what would happen in

00:10:09.291 --> 00:10:11.010
this very different
auction format

00:10:11.011 --> 00:10:12.875
and predict very
successfully there.

00:10:12.876 --> 00:10:15.975
So that's most model's economics

00:10:15.976 --> 00:10:17.200
would not be able to pass a test

00:10:17.201 --> 00:10:19.485
like that and it was
really exciting to be able

00:10:19.486 --> 00:10:22.005
to to do those kinds of
counterfactual predictions.

00:10:22.006 --> 00:10:26.825
&gt;&gt; So, this idea
of we have models,

00:10:26.826 --> 00:10:28.950
we did the model,

00:10:28.951 --> 00:10:32.495
some set of assumptions
about the world,

00:10:32.496 --> 00:10:34.305
we run them in the world.

00:10:34.306 --> 00:10:36.094
We may or may not see

00:10:36.095 --> 00:10:37.805
a challenge as they
have or problems they

00:10:37.806 --> 00:10:41.890
have from our evidence stream
that we have in place.

00:10:41.891 --> 00:10:44.190
We might later find
a bug or a problem,

00:10:44.191 --> 00:10:46.080
even when that's
been longstanding,

00:10:46.081 --> 00:10:49.423
yet exhaustion as to our models,

00:10:49.424 --> 00:10:50.970
and or understanding
of the system,

00:10:50.971 --> 00:10:53.065
and then we have to go
back and cycle in and

00:10:53.066 --> 00:10:55.770
somehow repair or come up with
new theories to fix that.

00:10:55.771 --> 00:10:56.980
I'm curious how
often that happens

00:10:56.981 --> 00:10:58.660
in economics these days.

00:10:58.661 --> 00:11:02.785
Is it less as we deploy
these systems in

00:11:02.786 --> 00:11:06.660
more or less ways
and observe and then

00:11:06.661 --> 00:11:08.320
refine them or is
it we still find

00:11:08.321 --> 00:11:10.870
funding once in
a while a major gaffe.

00:11:10.871 --> 00:11:12.995
Oh! My gosh. That
doesn't work very well.

00:11:12.996 --> 00:11:14.825
&gt;&gt; Well, I would
say that models are

00:11:14.826 --> 00:11:17.930
the macroeconomy generally
don't work very well.

00:11:17.931 --> 00:11:19.065
&gt;&gt; So we're in good shape.

00:11:19.066 --> 00:11:20.935
&gt;&gt; We have a long way to go.

00:11:20.936 --> 00:11:23.040
It's not the people's fault,

00:11:23.041 --> 00:11:24.890
it's just that at some level

00:11:24.891 --> 00:11:26.800
to learn something from data.

00:11:26.801 --> 00:11:28.985
It's most useful if you have

00:11:28.986 --> 00:11:31.865
experimental stimulus
or you can tweak

00:11:31.866 --> 00:11:34.090
one part and hold the other
conditions fixed and in

00:11:34.091 --> 00:11:37.285
the macroeconomy it's
just very hard to do so.

00:11:37.286 --> 00:11:39.290
But in microeconomic settings,

00:11:39.291 --> 00:11:40.495
like in this timber
auction setting,

00:11:40.496 --> 00:11:43.785
they literally did
an experiment and randomized,

00:11:43.786 --> 00:11:46.605
which auctions were
first prize seal bid

00:11:46.606 --> 00:11:47.990
and which ones were
open ascending.

00:11:47.991 --> 00:11:49.420
So, you really had

00:11:49.421 --> 00:11:50.960
that experimental variation and

00:11:50.961 --> 00:11:52.775
you could use that
to test the models.

00:11:52.776 --> 00:11:55.460
We did try different models
to make sure that we had

00:11:55.461 --> 00:11:58.690
ones that did the best job
both in sample,

00:11:58.691 --> 00:12:01.170
but also in counterfactual
predictions.

00:12:01.171 --> 00:12:04.360
&gt;&gt; They say or it's
just the issue of can we take

00:12:04.361 --> 00:12:06.620
this whole experience
and new model to

00:12:06.621 --> 00:12:08.945
another domain and see if it
still has the same effects?

00:12:08.946 --> 00:12:10.640
&gt;&gt; Well, so you would
build a different model.

00:12:10.641 --> 00:12:12.450
So the option models
are very specific.

00:12:12.451 --> 00:12:15.630
Everything about it is specific
to that particular game.

00:12:15.631 --> 00:12:17.370
So certainly, that literature,

00:12:17.371 --> 00:12:19.940
we've been doing empirical
models of auctions since

00:12:19.941 --> 00:12:21.480
the 1980s and so

00:12:21.481 --> 00:12:23.515
they've gotten better
and better and better.

00:12:23.516 --> 00:12:26.015
Broadly, I think the paradigm
has stayed the same,

00:12:26.016 --> 00:12:29.540
but we've gotten more flexible
estimation techniques

00:12:29.541 --> 00:12:31.840
and we've incorporated
more richness in them.

00:12:31.841 --> 00:12:33.390
But you're also limited by

00:12:33.391 --> 00:12:35.060
the variation that's in the data.

00:12:35.061 --> 00:12:37.345
So another thing
that economics puts

00:12:37.346 --> 00:12:40.670
a lot of emphasis on
is causal inference.

00:12:40.671 --> 00:12:43.730
Usually, the the main use case of

00:12:43.731 --> 00:12:45.610
an empirical economic model

00:12:45.611 --> 00:12:47.900
is to do some counterfactual.

00:12:47.901 --> 00:12:49.270
You want to understand,

00:12:49.271 --> 00:12:50.575
in microeconomics, what was

00:12:50.576 --> 00:12:51.890
the effect of the intervention?

00:12:51.891 --> 00:12:52.900
What was the effect of raising

00:12:52.901 --> 00:12:55.690
the minimum wage What would
happen if I changed my price?

00:12:55.691 --> 00:12:57.420
&gt; What would happen if kind of

00:12:57.421 --> 00:12:59.440
notions of the counterfactual?

00:12:59.441 --> 00:13:01.770
&gt;&gt; Exactly, and so
for many years,

00:13:01.771 --> 00:13:04.030
we've had to actually
do heroic things.

00:13:04.031 --> 00:13:06.285
So, we've had to figure out

00:13:06.286 --> 00:13:08.835
what would happen if
two firms merge and so

00:13:08.836 --> 00:13:11.010
a key and a typical
expert witness thing for

00:13:11.011 --> 00:13:12.735
an empirical economist would be

00:13:12.736 --> 00:13:15.980
to estimate demand for products,

00:13:15.981 --> 00:13:18.010
then say what if two firms

00:13:18.011 --> 00:13:19.890
merged and they maximize profits,

00:13:19.891 --> 00:13:21.580
how much would they raise prices?

00:13:21.581 --> 00:13:23.510
So these are all
about worlds that

00:13:23.511 --> 00:13:25.390
we've literally
never seen before.

00:13:25.391 --> 00:13:26.875
Those models, of course,

00:13:26.876 --> 00:13:29.190
are controversial because if
they've never seen before,

00:13:29.191 --> 00:13:31.205
when I used to explain these
to computer scientists,

00:13:31.206 --> 00:13:34.545
how can you build models when
you can't validate them?

00:13:34.546 --> 00:13:36.140
This is crazy. You guys were

00:13:36.141 --> 00:13:38.290
really even scientists
if you do that.

00:13:38.291 --> 00:13:40.065
Part of the answer is well,

00:13:40.066 --> 00:13:43.140
the Department of Justice
has to make a decision,

00:13:43.210 --> 00:13:45.460
I'm sorry but I can't have

00:13:45.461 --> 00:13:47.335
the firms merge and
then unmerge them.

00:13:47.336 --> 00:13:49.660
So I really do need
to make decisions.

00:13:49.661 --> 00:13:52.375
But I would say that one of
the themes of my research

00:13:52.376 --> 00:13:55.400
which has really
been reinforced by

00:13:55.401 --> 00:13:57.410
starting to interact
with computer scientists

00:13:57.411 --> 00:13:59.530
is that we should

00:13:59.531 --> 00:14:01.440
find ways to validate
our models and yes,

00:14:01.441 --> 00:14:02.980
we can't make the firms merge,

00:14:02.981 --> 00:14:05.075
but there may be
things in the data.

00:14:05.076 --> 00:14:06.535
&gt;&gt; That you can
generalize them somehow.

00:14:06.536 --> 00:14:08.390
&gt;&gt; Yes. We can look
at some things.

00:14:08.391 --> 00:14:09.890
We're not exactly like that. That

00:14:09.891 --> 00:14:12.000
are held out data
that's a little bit

00:14:12.001 --> 00:14:14.460
different than the data you
used to estimate and see

00:14:14.461 --> 00:14:16.955
how well you do for
those held out situations.

00:14:16.956 --> 00:14:19.550
&gt;&gt; Speaking of this,
we've seen rise

00:14:19.551 --> 00:14:23.955
of data-centric methods,

00:14:23.956 --> 00:14:25.620
their advances in
machine learning,

00:14:25.621 --> 00:14:27.150
the ability to predict with

00:14:27.151 --> 00:14:29.930
confidence scores
of various kinds.

00:14:30.030 --> 00:14:34.040
Comment a little bit
about the rise in

00:14:34.041 --> 00:14:35.580
uses of machine learning in

00:14:35.581 --> 00:14:37.745
economics and economic modeling.

00:14:37.746 --> 00:14:42.320
&gt;&gt; So, this is still fairly
Nietzschean economics,

00:14:42.321 --> 00:14:43.920
but I feel like it's on the verge

00:14:43.921 --> 00:14:45.705
of exploding and really,

00:14:45.706 --> 00:14:47.580
if I had to thank Microsoft for

00:14:47.581 --> 00:14:50.275
one and many things I
thank Microsoft for,

00:14:50.276 --> 00:14:51.580
one of them is that this is

00:14:51.581 --> 00:14:52.950
where I learned about
machine learning,

00:14:52.951 --> 00:14:55.300
and I was introduced
to machine learning by

00:14:55.301 --> 00:14:57.040
Microsoft Research
researchers who

00:14:57.041 --> 00:14:59.005
were working in
the Bing search engine.

00:14:59.006 --> 00:15:01.285
So, I think I was
one of the first,

00:15:01.286 --> 00:15:03.505
if not the first economist,

00:15:03.506 --> 00:15:05.625
leading economist to say,

00:15:05.626 --> 00:15:07.525
"Hey, this is really big."

00:15:07.526 --> 00:15:08.760
I think that there are things

00:15:08.761 --> 00:15:10.115
about machine learning that you

00:15:10.116 --> 00:15:11.350
can bring into economics

00:15:11.351 --> 00:15:12.715
that would really make
things different.

00:15:12.716 --> 00:15:15.820
I should mention that in
our first interactions.

00:15:15.821 --> 00:15:18.110
It was this strange thing

00:15:18.111 --> 00:15:20.385
where I was an
economist and I said

00:15:20.386 --> 00:15:23.610
economics has been using
data to make decisions

00:15:23.611 --> 00:15:27.285
and do analysis for
decades and really,

00:15:27.286 --> 00:15:29.320
we have never imported
anything from

00:15:29.321 --> 00:15:31.190
computer science into doing this.

00:15:31.191 --> 00:15:34.400
We're expert witnesses, we
run government agencies,

00:15:34.401 --> 00:15:38.475
we work on drugs in school
class size and minimum wages.

00:15:38.476 --> 00:15:41.470
Computer science isn't
even part of this conversation.

00:15:41.471 --> 00:15:43.180
How come you think
that you actually

00:15:43.181 --> 00:15:44.490
have a way to do this better.

00:15:44.491 --> 00:15:45.890
Then the computer
scientists were like,

00:15:45.891 --> 00:15:47.435
we're running
a whole search engine

00:15:47.436 --> 00:15:49.005
like there's no economists here,

00:15:49.006 --> 00:15:51.170
we've never learned anything
about econometrics.

00:15:51.171 --> 00:15:54.020
How could this possibly
have anything to teach us?

00:15:54.021 --> 00:15:56.350
Actually, what's been
really exciting is that

00:15:56.351 --> 00:15:58.320
in the last 10 years both sides,

00:15:58.321 --> 00:16:00.080
I think, have really
realized that there are

00:16:00.081 --> 00:16:02.135
some interesting things to learn.

00:16:02.136 --> 00:16:04.925
&gt;&gt; What are some of
the, in your mind,

00:16:04.926 --> 00:16:06.500
some of the key touch points or

00:16:06.501 --> 00:16:08.740
synthesis opportunities that are

00:16:08.741 --> 00:16:11.160
coming to the fore
with computer science,

00:16:11.161 --> 00:16:12.900
particularly machine
learning, artificial

00:16:12.901 --> 00:16:15.520
intelligence more
broadly in economics?

00:16:15.521 --> 00:16:18.735
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I think economics
has always been good at it.

00:16:18.736 --> 00:16:20.970
There are really laser focused on

00:16:20.971 --> 00:16:23.390
counterfactual prediction
cause and effect,

00:16:23.391 --> 00:16:24.910
what if questions,

00:16:24.911 --> 00:16:27.330
but always like in
a small data framework.

00:16:27.331 --> 00:16:29.035
So you assume, roughly you have

00:16:29.036 --> 00:16:32.100
three covariates and
a million observations.

00:16:32.101 --> 00:16:36.105
So, we worked on
flexible models but in

00:16:36.106 --> 00:16:37.865
a very small data like

00:16:37.866 --> 00:16:40.650
a very small number of
covariates setting.

00:16:40.651 --> 00:16:43.515
We pretended that God
gave us our model,

00:16:43.516 --> 00:16:47.500
the god of economics gave
us the functional form and

00:16:47.501 --> 00:16:49.410
actually data mining was

00:16:49.411 --> 00:16:51.860
a pejorative term that

00:16:51.861 --> 00:16:54.645
was like what people
did that were cheating.

00:16:54.646 --> 00:16:57.215
It was like a synonym
for P-value hacking.

00:16:57.216 --> 00:16:59.990
P-value hacking
meaning keep testing

00:16:59.991 --> 00:17:01.660
things over and over
again until you

00:17:01.661 --> 00:17:04.135
find something that
fits your hypothesis.

00:17:04.136 --> 00:17:07.075
So, what I feel

00:17:07.076 --> 00:17:09.830
we've really imported very
well from machine learning in

00:17:09.831 --> 00:17:12.440
economics is
this data-driven search

00:17:12.441 --> 00:17:14.615
for models and
much richer classes

00:17:14.616 --> 00:17:17.885
of models and much better
more systematic ways

00:17:17.886 --> 00:17:20.080
to fit the data well.

00:17:20.081 --> 00:17:24.470
Where I feel that
machine learning was,

00:17:24.471 --> 00:17:25.460
there's always been people

00:17:25.461 --> 00:17:26.430
in machine learning who thought

00:17:26.431 --> 00:17:28.610
about friends people
like Judea Pearl,

00:17:28.611 --> 00:17:30.075
but it was really
fairly Nietzsche

00:17:30.076 --> 00:17:31.545
and you would meet
machine learning people

00:17:31.546 --> 00:17:32.980
who really weren't very

00:17:32.981 --> 00:17:35.530
conversant in
the framework of it.

00:17:35.531 --> 00:17:37.970
You could finish a PhD
in Computer Science

00:17:37.971 --> 00:17:40.605
and mostly think about,

00:17:40.606 --> 00:17:42.240
you'd learn mostly
about prediction

00:17:42.241 --> 00:17:43.420
and so you would come.

00:17:43.421 --> 00:17:45.885
&gt;&gt; Even that even describes

00:17:45.886 --> 00:17:49.135
Judea Pearl's left until
1988 or so as I recall.

00:17:49.136 --> 00:17:51.465
&gt;&gt; Yeah, but still today
a lot of people come out

00:17:51.466 --> 00:17:53.815
and talk come out of a PhD
and they've in some sense,

00:17:53.816 --> 00:17:55.100
they've learned about prediction,

00:17:55.101 --> 00:17:57.430
about fitting data and then

00:17:57.431 --> 00:17:58.735
if you have this hammer

00:17:58.736 --> 00:18:00.210
you feel like
everything's a nail.

00:18:00.211 --> 00:18:01.470
But if you take something

00:18:01.471 --> 00:18:05.095
like trying to do quick
prediction in the search engine.

00:18:05.096 --> 00:18:08.360
In fact, we're doing
some counterfactual predictions,

00:18:08.361 --> 00:18:09.660
because we're asking
the question,

00:18:09.661 --> 00:18:11.480
what would happen if
I took something from

00:18:11.481 --> 00:18:15.050
the third position and put
it into the first position.

00:18:15.051 --> 00:18:18.910
So, you're actually doing
a what if that changes

00:18:18.911 --> 00:18:20.660
the environment away from the one

00:18:20.661 --> 00:18:22.910
that you used to fit the data.

00:18:22.911 --> 00:18:25.970
I think a lot of the tech firms

00:18:25.971 --> 00:18:27.400
will talk about things

00:18:27.401 --> 00:18:29.080
as if they are doing
counterfactuals,

00:18:29.081 --> 00:18:30.775
but the algorithms themselves

00:18:30.776 --> 00:18:33.140
didn't account for that
and they were just fitted.

00:18:33.141 --> 00:18:33.735
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:18:33.736 --> 00:18:35.200
&gt;&gt; It's interesting.
Sorry I chopped up, but

00:18:35.201 --> 00:18:36.760
actually you got me excited
here about thinking

00:18:36.761 --> 00:18:38.410
about some of my conversations

00:18:38.411 --> 00:18:40.225
with you to Pearl directly.

00:18:40.226 --> 00:18:45.075
It's been interesting how
passionate he is about

00:18:45.076 --> 00:18:48.565
the core capability in

00:18:48.566 --> 00:18:51.110
any intelligence system to
be a human with one's we're

00:18:51.111 --> 00:18:53.890
trying to build to do
counterfactual reasoning,

00:18:53.891 --> 00:18:55.590
to imagine, to envision

00:18:55.591 --> 00:18:57.960
possibilities that
don't exist today,

00:18:57.961 --> 00:18:59.650
don't exist here and now,

00:18:59.651 --> 00:19:02.130
and to imagine manipulations,

00:19:02.131 --> 00:19:05.305
actions, sequences of action
that would change the world.

00:19:05.306 --> 00:19:07.360
The fact that all civilization

00:19:07.361 --> 00:19:09.140
cannot come out of
that capability,

00:19:09.141 --> 00:19:11.045
a pop of civilization came from

00:19:11.046 --> 00:19:14.210
the special human ability
to do that well.

00:19:14.211 --> 00:19:18.365
Yet largely, we see today an AI,

00:19:18.366 --> 00:19:21.500
prediction technologies as
well as applied in business,

00:19:21.501 --> 00:19:23.440
prediction technologies
and that's been

00:19:23.441 --> 00:19:25.860
referred to as the AI
revolution in many places.

00:19:25.861 --> 00:19:27.840
You've comment about that?

00:19:27.841 --> 00:19:30.690
&gt;&gt; Yes. So, I think
that was really why

00:19:30.691 --> 00:19:33.585
I got excited about
working about on AI,

00:19:33.586 --> 00:19:35.640
why I thought I had something
to bring to the table.

00:19:35.641 --> 00:19:39.000
Because all of my training
was about counterfactuals,

00:19:39.001 --> 00:19:40.745
but it was applied to

00:19:40.746 --> 00:19:45.160
very simple datasets and
really simple problems

00:19:45.161 --> 00:19:48.595
with these huge new data sets
as well as opportunities in

00:19:48.596 --> 00:19:50.260
tech firms to intervene and run

00:19:50.261 --> 00:19:51.940
lots of experiments
and things like that.

00:19:51.941 --> 00:19:54.210
There was a possibility to learn

00:19:54.211 --> 00:19:57.840
about cause and effect
in a much grander scale.

00:19:57.841 --> 00:20:00.450
So, I want the theoretical

00:20:00.451 --> 00:20:03.370
insights from people
like Judea Pearl or

00:20:03.371 --> 00:20:06.560
Don Rubin that set

00:20:06.561 --> 00:20:09.910
up a framework for
thinking about this.

00:20:09.911 --> 00:20:12.725
Those are those theoretical
insights can be

00:20:12.726 --> 00:20:16.105
implemented in a much richer way,

00:20:16.106 --> 00:20:19.410
when you combine
the computational techniques and

00:20:19.411 --> 00:20:21.025
the estimation techniques from

00:20:21.026 --> 00:20:24.360
machine learning to do
things like personalization,

00:20:24.361 --> 00:20:27.895
and to control for
lots of co-variates,

00:20:27.896 --> 00:20:31.840
but still maintaining
the rigorous theoretical focus

00:20:31.841 --> 00:20:33.280
on cause and effect.

00:20:33.281 --> 00:20:34.995
So, just as an example,

00:20:34.996 --> 00:20:38.575
in old style economics
we might have said,

00:20:38.576 --> 00:20:41.615
let's look at the average
effective changing price.

00:20:41.616 --> 00:20:44.200
Then we might run an experiment

00:20:44.201 --> 00:20:46.430
with price changes or it
might be that there's

00:20:46.431 --> 00:20:48.870
some natural experiment in
the past something that caused

00:20:48.871 --> 00:20:50.150
the price to pay change that was

00:20:50.151 --> 00:20:52.165
unrelated to consumer demand,

00:20:52.166 --> 00:20:53.915
but we would estimate
an average effect.

00:20:53.916 --> 00:20:55.280
Machine learning
has gotten really

00:20:55.281 --> 00:20:57.100
good at personalized predictions.

00:20:57.101 --> 00:21:00.100
So, you can adapt a lot
of the algorithms from

00:21:00.101 --> 00:21:02.245
personalized prediction

00:21:02.246 --> 00:21:03.690
or just personalized
fitting, like,

00:21:03.691 --> 00:21:04.930
saying okay which of these people

00:21:04.931 --> 00:21:06.340
is going to click or which of

00:21:06.341 --> 00:21:09.715
these people is going to buy,

00:21:09.716 --> 00:21:11.910
and then adapt those techniques

00:21:11.911 --> 00:21:13.840
to answer the
question for which of

00:21:13.841 --> 00:21:15.985
these people will
a price change cause

00:21:15.986 --> 00:21:18.550
them not to buy or
cause them to buy.

00:21:18.551 --> 00:21:22.230
So, it's not a huge leap
computationally,

00:21:22.231 --> 00:21:24.295
and algorithmically
from one to the other,

00:21:24.296 --> 00:21:27.085
but there are also
some key insights

00:21:27.086 --> 00:21:29.675
that are important to
recognize when you get there.

00:21:29.676 --> 00:21:32.490
So, let me, when

00:21:32.491 --> 00:21:33.680
we talk about this
in economics is

00:21:33.681 --> 00:21:35.000
we call it identification.

00:21:35.001 --> 00:21:37.365
We want to know, if I
had an infinite dataset

00:21:37.366 --> 00:21:40.750
could I possibly
answer my question?

00:21:40.890 --> 00:21:44.075
For causal questions, if you have

00:21:44.076 --> 00:21:48.140
a randomized experiment
that varied the treatment,

00:21:48.141 --> 00:21:49.960
then it's pretty clear that I can

00:21:49.961 --> 00:21:51.395
learn about cause and effect,

00:21:51.396 --> 00:21:53.355
but what if I don't?

00:21:53.356 --> 00:21:56.970
There's a lot of question
of work in economics about

00:21:56.971 --> 00:21:58.410
what you could do
if you didn't have

00:21:58.411 --> 00:22:00.720
an actual randomized experiment.

00:22:00.721 --> 00:22:03.560
But there's still
something in the data that

00:22:03.561 --> 00:22:05.140
varied that could be used to

00:22:05.141 --> 00:22:06.995
find the causal effect anyway,

00:22:06.996 --> 00:22:09.850
but it's very delicate
getting that done.

00:22:09.851 --> 00:22:12.870
&gt;&gt; How do we deal with these days

00:22:12.871 --> 00:22:15.835
in economics with causation
under uncertainty?

00:22:15.836 --> 00:22:17.770
We have a problem with
distribution over

00:22:17.771 --> 00:22:21.240
a causal effect or
the impact of a cause.

00:22:22.700 --> 00:22:25.780
&gt;&gt; So, there are different
branches of dealing with that.

00:22:25.781 --> 00:22:28.195
So, in the classical approaches,

00:22:28.196 --> 00:22:30.420
we would estimate a model and

00:22:30.421 --> 00:22:32.680
there would be a confidence
interval around the effect.

00:22:32.681 --> 00:22:35.275
So I would estimate
a price elasticity.

00:22:35.276 --> 00:22:37.430
Price elasticity means how

00:22:37.431 --> 00:22:41.540
responsive is say a market
to a price change.

00:22:41.541 --> 00:22:45.210
Then, I would try to get
an unbiased estimate

00:22:45.211 --> 00:22:46.900
of that price elasticity

00:22:46.901 --> 00:22:49.225
and put a confidence
interval around it.

00:22:49.226 --> 00:22:52.180
A lot of the machine
learning models actually

00:22:52.181 --> 00:22:55.270
don't have good statistical
properties and you wouldn't

00:22:55.271 --> 00:22:58.090
necessarily be able
to get an estimate of

00:22:58.091 --> 00:22:59.410
a parameter out of

00:22:59.411 --> 00:23:01.265
those models that
centered on the truth.

00:23:01.266 --> 00:23:02.870
So, one of the focuses

00:23:02.871 --> 00:23:04.810
of my theoretical
research has been

00:23:04.811 --> 00:23:08.125
to provide the
statistical foundation

00:23:08.126 --> 00:23:10.210
for popular machine
learning models.

00:23:10.211 --> 00:23:13.570
I focused on random forests
because they are amenable to

00:23:13.571 --> 00:23:15.810
statistical analysis
and they work pretty

00:23:15.811 --> 00:23:18.690
well for a class of problems.

00:23:18.691 --> 00:23:20.760
&gt;&gt; As others might
say, you can always do

00:23:20.761 --> 00:23:22.530
bootstrap to get
some statistics and

00:23:22.531 --> 00:23:24.540
some properties over even models

00:23:24.541 --> 00:23:26.815
that don't have the foundational
properties you want.

00:23:26.816 --> 00:23:28.680
&gt;&gt; Yeah, exactly. So,
I mean and, of course,

00:23:28.681 --> 00:23:32.530
neural nets have eluded a lot
of theoretical analysis.

00:23:32.531 --> 00:23:35.850
But actually, for some problems
that are a little bit

00:23:35.851 --> 00:23:37.700
simpler like
economic data sets where

00:23:37.701 --> 00:23:39.855
you might have [inaudible]
, your curvature,

00:23:39.856 --> 00:23:41.740
where we have modified

00:23:41.741 --> 00:23:43.660
versions of random forests
that do things like

00:23:43.661 --> 00:23:48.810
bias correction or combine
linear models with

00:23:48.811 --> 00:23:51.540
the random forest that will do

00:23:51.541 --> 00:23:53.230
a pretty good
job fitting data that's

00:23:53.231 --> 00:23:55.575
not quite as complex
as like images.

00:23:55.576 --> 00:23:58.415
Then, we've provided
the statistical guarantees

00:23:58.416 --> 00:24:00.680
so that people can then
get what they want,

00:24:00.681 --> 00:24:02.880
a parameter estimate and
a confidence interval.

00:24:02.881 --> 00:24:04.510
We also have a tradition of

00:24:04.511 --> 00:24:06.500
Bayesian modeling in economics,

00:24:06.501 --> 00:24:09.250
which is always been
more niche but that tradition-

00:24:09.251 --> 00:24:10.460
&gt;&gt; That's unfortunate.

00:24:10.461 --> 00:24:12.660
&gt;&gt; -actually meets very well

00:24:12.661 --> 00:24:14.290
with the computer science
tradition, and actually,

00:24:14.291 --> 00:24:15.805
the work I'm going
to present today,

00:24:15.806 --> 00:24:18.060
it feels is in
the Bayesian tradition.

00:24:18.061 --> 00:24:19.230
I'm working with David Bly,

00:24:19.231 --> 00:24:20.980
who's a computer
scientist at Columbia.

00:24:20.981 --> 00:24:21.585
&gt;&gt; He is a friend?

00:24:21.586 --> 00:24:22.800
&gt;&gt; Yeah. He's gotten very

00:24:22.801 --> 00:24:24.240
interested in
these Causal Models.

00:24:24.241 --> 00:24:26.470
So, Bayesian models are actually

00:24:26.471 --> 00:24:29.370
very amenable to
more complex counterfactuals,

00:24:29.371 --> 00:24:31.350
because you're generally writing

00:24:31.351 --> 00:24:32.995
down a data generating process,

00:24:32.996 --> 00:24:35.090
and so that data
generating process in

00:24:35.091 --> 00:24:37.360
some sense is a model
of how the world works.

00:24:37.361 --> 00:24:38.275
&gt;&gt; Exactly.

00:24:38.276 --> 00:24:41.080
&gt;&gt; So, coming back to
your question about AI,

00:24:41.081 --> 00:24:43.610
let me elaborate on
that a little bit more.

00:24:43.611 --> 00:24:46.180
Economics has
sort of two categories

00:24:46.181 --> 00:24:48.320
of counterfactuals
that we might do.

00:24:48.321 --> 00:24:49.995
One it's like
there's a treatment,

00:24:49.996 --> 00:24:52.845
a minimum wage, or
even a price increase.

00:24:52.846 --> 00:24:55.265
Some people in the data
have experienced

00:24:55.266 --> 00:24:57.280
that change and
we're just trying to

00:24:57.281 --> 00:24:58.670
figure out what would
happen if everybody

00:24:58.671 --> 00:24:59.750
experienced the change,

00:24:59.751 --> 00:25:02.079
or what was the comparison

00:25:02.080 --> 00:25:04.010
between people who had the
change and people who didn't.

00:25:04.011 --> 00:25:05.150
But it's always something that's

00:25:05.151 --> 00:25:06.520
actually happened in
the world and that's

00:25:06.521 --> 00:25:07.720
why the program evaluation

00:25:07.721 --> 00:25:09.115
or treatment effect literature.

00:25:09.116 --> 00:25:10.520
There's another literature which

00:25:10.521 --> 00:25:12.330
tries to be more ambitious,

00:25:12.331 --> 00:25:14.515
but also requires
more assumptions.

00:25:14.516 --> 00:25:16.240
This is the literature
that would say what

00:25:16.241 --> 00:25:18.070
would happen if two firms merged?

00:25:18.071 --> 00:25:19.485
What would happen if I raised

00:25:19.486 --> 00:25:21.770
a price that has never
been charged before?

00:25:21.771 --> 00:25:25.260
So, of course, you have to
have some extrapolation.

00:25:25.261 --> 00:25:28.380
If for things like
raising prices though,

00:25:28.381 --> 00:25:30.630
maybe the extrapolation
isn't too crazy.

00:25:30.631 --> 00:25:31.860
If you could really learn about

00:25:31.861 --> 00:25:34.100
consumer preferences from
seeing the choices they

00:25:34.101 --> 00:25:38.250
make and you
extrapolate carefully,

00:25:38.251 --> 00:25:41.890
you can have a pretty grounded
view of what would

00:25:41.891 --> 00:25:43.715
happen if the price

00:25:43.716 --> 00:25:45.465
went someplace you've
never seen before.

00:25:45.466 --> 00:25:46.920
So, a model.

00:25:46.921 --> 00:25:49.375
So, you need more functional
form assumptions for that.

00:25:49.376 --> 00:25:51.985
Because if you're just feeding
in a dataset you have,

00:25:51.986 --> 00:25:55.260
things can go wildly wrong
without any functional.

00:25:55.261 --> 00:25:56.660
If you don't have
any functional form assumptions,

00:25:56.661 --> 00:25:58.080
things can go wildly wrong if you

00:25:58.081 --> 00:25:59.925
go outside of
the range of the data.

00:25:59.926 --> 00:26:01.935
While these sort of
more structured models

00:26:01.936 --> 00:26:03.380
that build up and say,

00:26:03.381 --> 00:26:05.140
I'm going to estimate for

00:26:05.141 --> 00:26:07.625
each consumer based on
their past behavior,

00:26:07.626 --> 00:26:09.710
how they feel about prices.

00:26:09.711 --> 00:26:13.140
Then I can extrapolate to

00:26:13.141 --> 00:26:14.630
a situation where I present

00:26:14.631 --> 00:26:16.885
the consumer with another choice.

00:26:16.886 --> 00:26:19.880
Similarly, we can model
firm profit functions.

00:26:19.881 --> 00:26:22.780
If we can understand
the parameters

00:26:22.781 --> 00:26:24.160
of a firm profit function and we

00:26:24.161 --> 00:26:25.875
assume they maximize profits,

00:26:25.876 --> 00:26:27.810
then I can evaluate
what they would

00:26:27.811 --> 00:26:29.930
do in a situation they
haven't been in before.

00:26:29.931 --> 00:26:31.130
Or better in an auction,

00:26:31.131 --> 00:26:32.655
if I understand
their preferences,

00:26:32.656 --> 00:26:34.755
I can understand how they
would play a different game.

00:26:34.756 --> 00:26:38.640
&gt;&gt; Thinking of preferences,
in the mid-90s,

00:26:38.641 --> 00:26:42.310
I and colleagues worked
on recommender engines,

00:26:42.311 --> 00:26:44.050
even Bayesian versions of this.

00:26:44.051 --> 00:26:46.860
The idea was, we would assume

00:26:46.861 --> 00:26:50.210
static preferences of the users
coming into a service,

00:26:50.211 --> 00:26:56.410
for example, and we would
post this for assessment.

00:26:56.411 --> 00:26:58.180
Movies, for example.

00:26:58.181 --> 00:27:00.910
Like this movie; yes/no, yes/no,

00:27:00.911 --> 00:27:03.220
to come up with
a probability distribution

00:27:03.221 --> 00:27:05.250
over their preferences and,

00:27:05.251 --> 00:27:08.155
of course, items that they
would prefer or choose.

00:27:08.156 --> 00:27:10.270
We would use dive
information application.

00:27:10.271 --> 00:27:13.100
If we say, here was
the next most valuable item

00:27:13.101 --> 00:27:15.360
to assess, to maximum,

00:27:15.361 --> 00:27:16.795
we get to the core of

00:27:16.796 --> 00:27:19.130
static preferences
without worrying

00:27:19.131 --> 00:27:21.385
about causality at
all in those days.

00:27:21.386 --> 00:27:24.840
I'm guessing and I know
for certain companies,

00:27:24.841 --> 00:27:27.270
this is still the way
recommender systems work.

00:27:27.271 --> 00:27:29.345
You might like to buy this book

00:27:29.346 --> 00:27:32.485
versus doing
richer causal reasoning.

00:27:32.486 --> 00:27:35.280
You could critique the old way

00:27:35.281 --> 00:27:37.750
and the assumptions versus
what you do today if you

00:27:37.751 --> 00:27:40.380
had unlimited time to

00:27:40.381 --> 00:27:43.465
assessed and unlimited
computing data.

00:27:43.466 --> 00:27:46.955
&gt;&gt; So, I actually think
that the connections

00:27:46.956 --> 00:27:49.100
between what you were talking

00:27:49.101 --> 00:27:51.410
about there and what
we're doing today,

00:27:51.411 --> 00:27:53.915
I'm promoting today are tight.

00:27:53.916 --> 00:27:56.335
I think in some sense like
Bayesian are Bayesian,

00:27:56.336 --> 00:27:58.700
whether they are economists
or computer scientists to

00:27:58.701 --> 00:28:00.050
some level because you

00:28:00.051 --> 00:28:02.450
are writing down
a generative model of the world.

00:28:02.451 --> 00:28:02.795
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:28:02.796 --> 00:28:04.510
&gt;&gt; That is basically
what economists do.

00:28:04.511 --> 00:28:05.990
They write down
generative models of

00:28:05.991 --> 00:28:07.870
the world which
impose assumptions.

00:28:07.871 --> 00:28:08.650
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:28:08.651 --> 00:28:09.880
&gt;&gt; I think if you're going to

00:28:09.881 --> 00:28:11.580
extrapolate, you
need assumptions.

00:28:11.581 --> 00:28:14.550
I think that's really
a core point for AI.

00:28:14.551 --> 00:28:15.980
If you want an AI to work in

00:28:15.981 --> 00:28:18.305
the environment you
have, that's awesome.

00:28:18.306 --> 00:28:20.380
If you want the AI to do anything

00:28:20.381 --> 00:28:22.630
outside of the
environment experienced,

00:28:22.631 --> 00:28:25.315
then suddenly you
really need models,

00:28:25.316 --> 00:28:27.210
because you need to know how

00:28:27.211 --> 00:28:29.450
to extrapolate from
what you've learned.

00:28:29.451 --> 00:28:31.050
So, I would say that I think that

00:28:31.051 --> 00:28:35.400
recommender literature
is actually great.

00:28:35.401 --> 00:28:36.530
You're writing down
a generative model,

00:28:36.531 --> 00:28:38.200
you're trying to learn
the parameters of the model.

00:28:38.201 --> 00:28:42.500
The things that we might add
in would be thinking harder

00:28:42.501 --> 00:28:44.570
about what variation in

00:28:44.571 --> 00:28:46.795
the data really allows
you to learn things.

00:28:46.796 --> 00:28:49.330
So, if you just had
historical log data

00:28:49.331 --> 00:28:53.035
and you were only using
that historical log data,

00:28:53.036 --> 00:28:54.970
it could be that the things
that people were

00:28:54.971 --> 00:28:58.015
shown in the past
were not accidental.

00:28:58.016 --> 00:29:01.880
Somebody was shown
something and they chose

00:29:01.881 --> 00:29:05.340
it because of the
characteristics of that person.

00:29:05.341 --> 00:29:06.465
&gt;&gt; Probably effect
and the sampling,

00:29:06.466 --> 00:29:08.180
basically that model you

00:29:08.181 --> 00:29:11.125
set an assumption in
the sampling procedure.

00:29:11.126 --> 00:29:14.725
&gt;&gt;Yeah, exactly. Then one
of the things economists

00:29:14.726 --> 00:29:16.750
or causal inference people in

00:29:16.751 --> 00:29:19.110
statistics might add
in to that is to say,

00:29:19.111 --> 00:29:21.230
okay you either needed to
actually only work from

00:29:21.231 --> 00:29:23.490
experimental data
where the choices that

00:29:23.491 --> 00:29:25.690
people saw were
randomized in some way

00:29:25.691 --> 00:29:28.715
to really understand
their preferences,

00:29:28.716 --> 00:29:34.690
or if the log data was
generated from a process,

00:29:34.691 --> 00:29:37.480
then you need to estimate
aspects of the process that

00:29:37.481 --> 00:29:40.790
generated the data to control
for the selection effects,

00:29:40.791 --> 00:29:44.335
to control for the fact that
some people picked book A,

00:29:44.336 --> 00:29:46.590
but that was because they
were never shown book D.

00:29:46.591 --> 00:29:47.950
Another people shown and picked

00:29:47.951 --> 00:29:50.150
the book D but they were
never shown the book A.

00:29:50.151 --> 00:29:51.900
So, you need to
correct for the fact

00:29:51.901 --> 00:29:53.400
that not everybody was shown

00:29:53.401 --> 00:29:56.985
the same thing in
making their choices.

00:29:56.986 --> 00:29:58.395
But to some extent,

00:29:58.396 --> 00:29:59.740
I think in some applications,

00:29:59.741 --> 00:30:02.270
you might have been working
with logged data that was

00:30:02.271 --> 00:30:03.730
fairly clean and those problems

00:30:03.731 --> 00:30:05.145
wouldn't make
that big a difference,

00:30:05.146 --> 00:30:06.990
while in other
settings like when I

00:30:06.991 --> 00:30:09.530
looked at this in Bing Ads,

00:30:09.531 --> 00:30:12.390
these problems are really big.

00:30:13.510 --> 00:30:15.580
Number two is with a scientist

00:30:15.581 --> 00:30:18.850
that makes up research
for a while and he

00:30:18.851 --> 00:30:20.620
was very interested in
these ideas as well

00:30:20.621 --> 00:30:22.600
and we work together on
some things and he did

00:30:22.601 --> 00:30:25.130
a bunch of additional work

00:30:25.131 --> 00:30:28.965
on showing the difference
between experimental estimates.

00:30:28.966 --> 00:30:31.740
He basically run experiments

00:30:31.741 --> 00:30:33.515
and so you could compare
what you got from

00:30:33.516 --> 00:30:35.885
purely experimental data
and from the log data

00:30:35.886 --> 00:30:38.540
using the machine learning
methods in practice,

00:30:38.541 --> 00:30:39.950
and you could show
and demonstrate

00:30:39.951 --> 00:30:41.620
that you got
the wrong answers from

00:30:41.621 --> 00:30:44.305
not taking additional steps

00:30:44.306 --> 00:30:45.620
to compensate for the fact

00:30:45.621 --> 00:30:47.320
that your data is
not experimental.

00:30:47.321 --> 00:30:49.310
So, I think we have great

00:30:49.311 --> 00:30:50.830
techniques for doing that kind of

00:30:50.831 --> 00:30:54.730
compensation in
small data settings.

00:30:54.731 --> 00:30:59.055
But I think we still don't
have them at the right scale.

00:30:59.056 --> 00:31:01.790
I think there's still
room for improvement and

00:31:01.791 --> 00:31:04.840
big recommendation systems
today because we haven't

00:31:04.841 --> 00:31:07.020
figured out how to fully
and optimally scale

00:31:07.021 --> 00:31:10.690
those techniques to
production size problems.

00:31:10.691 --> 00:31:14.930
The big change. Just to pave
it a bit on to careers,

00:31:14.931 --> 00:31:18.520
she seem to be very excited
about things you're doing.

00:31:18.521 --> 00:31:20.345
You love these ideas,

00:31:20.346 --> 00:31:25.945
and you've made a set of
career decisions over time,

00:31:25.946 --> 00:31:27.230
to bring you to this place.

00:31:27.231 --> 00:31:28.750
Of course for every person,

00:31:28.751 --> 00:31:30.360
we are all different and,

00:31:30.361 --> 00:31:33.075
we can do what is of course
for their own lives,

00:31:33.076 --> 00:31:35.810
and imagine what we might be.

00:31:35.811 --> 00:31:38.210
But you've got into
a really fabulous place.

00:31:38.211 --> 00:31:40.390
I think from
the outside looking in.

00:31:40.391 --> 00:31:42.970
I'm curious if you have
some career advice

00:31:42.971 --> 00:31:44.400
for others right now,

00:31:44.401 --> 00:31:46.570
as they do their
what is out there,

00:31:46.571 --> 00:31:48.620
the viewers watching this,

00:31:48.621 --> 00:31:49.885
they're thinking about careers,

00:31:49.886 --> 00:31:52.850
even in economics, or computer
science or related fields.

00:31:52.851 --> 00:31:56.290
&gt;&gt; Sure. So, I would say
the first 12 years in

00:31:56.291 --> 00:31:59.745
my career were very successful,

00:31:59.746 --> 00:32:02.275
but I was working in
a very mature field.

00:32:02.276 --> 00:32:04.500
So, I told some of
the timber auction work

00:32:04.501 --> 00:32:06.720
I talked about it was
beautifully executed.

00:32:06.721 --> 00:32:09.640
It laid some good role models for

00:32:09.641 --> 00:32:10.850
other people to think about

00:32:10.851 --> 00:32:13.005
validating the structural
models and so.

00:32:13.006 --> 00:32:14.670
But ultimately, I would say

00:32:14.671 --> 00:32:16.680
none of it was actually
path-breaking.

00:32:16.681 --> 00:32:19.415
Because the all the fields
that I was working in,

00:32:19.416 --> 00:32:21.560
had the paradigms were

00:32:21.561 --> 00:32:23.970
established when I was
born as an economist.

00:32:23.971 --> 00:32:26.845
I was always jealous
during that time period

00:32:26.846 --> 00:32:29.010
of the people in the really

00:32:29.011 --> 00:32:31.315
the 1980s had laid
the groundwork.

00:32:31.316 --> 00:32:33.040
The real conceptual
insights came in

00:32:33.041 --> 00:32:35.300
the 80s before I was
born as an economist.

00:32:35.301 --> 00:32:38.115
We could extend them and
expand them and improve them.

00:32:38.116 --> 00:32:40.895
But it wasn't didn't
really feel fundamental.

00:32:40.896 --> 00:32:42.610
So, actually when I got

00:32:42.611 --> 00:32:44.760
the call to come
work for Microsoft,

00:32:44.761 --> 00:32:45.800
I got a call said,
"Do you want to

00:32:45.801 --> 00:32:47.115
come meet with Steve Ballmer?"

00:32:47.116 --> 00:32:50.090
I said sure, and then he was
like "Yeah I think we need

00:32:50.091 --> 00:32:53.135
an economist to think
about this online stuff."

00:32:53.136 --> 00:32:55.350
I was like "Okay sure".

00:32:55.351 --> 00:32:56.785
&gt;&gt; This online stuff.

00:32:56.786 --> 00:32:58.360
&gt;&gt; Yeah. They're we're pretty

00:32:58.361 --> 00:32:59.840
good at Windows, I
know how to do that.

00:32:59.841 --> 00:33:00.620
I don't think we really

00:33:00.621 --> 00:33:02.540
understand how to
compete with Google.

00:33:02.541 --> 00:33:05.870
That was a crazy thing
for me to do.

00:33:05.871 --> 00:33:07.120
I was a professor at Harvard.

00:33:07.121 --> 00:33:09.500
I was I had just
gotten this big prize.

00:33:09.501 --> 00:33:11.290
Everybody loved what
I had done to date.

00:33:11.291 --> 00:33:14.030
It was I should have just kept
going in what I was doing.

00:33:14.031 --> 00:33:15.875
&gt;&gt; You said big prize,
but this is like

00:33:15.876 --> 00:33:19.580
the Nobel Prize in Economics
for a young economist.

00:33:19.581 --> 00:33:20.990
&gt;&gt; Yes it was, that's

00:33:20.991 --> 00:33:22.250
actually how Steve
found out about me.

00:33:22.251 --> 00:33:24.285
There was a big article
about me in the paper.

00:33:24.286 --> 00:33:27.200
So, the natural thing
to do would've been to

00:33:27.201 --> 00:33:29.835
just be great at
what I was great at.

00:33:29.836 --> 00:33:31.225
I really, I was working,

00:33:31.226 --> 00:33:33.440
at that point to find
the field as market design,

00:33:33.441 --> 00:33:35.885
which was bringing theory
and data together to solve

00:33:35.886 --> 00:33:37.100
auction problems and

00:33:37.101 --> 00:33:38.780
school choice problems
and things like that.

00:33:38.781 --> 00:33:40.900
I was well-recognized
as the young

00:33:40.901 --> 00:33:42.060
not the old leader but

00:33:42.061 --> 00:33:43.735
at least the leader
of my generation,

00:33:43.736 --> 00:33:45.285
a leader of
my generation in that.

00:33:45.286 --> 00:33:48.795
But then the opportunity
came to come to Microsoft,

00:33:48.796 --> 00:33:50.700
and I just, was just,

00:33:50.701 --> 00:33:53.750
seduced by this idea of,

00:33:53.751 --> 00:33:55.410
I went in and I looked
at the search engine,

00:33:55.411 --> 00:33:58.110
and it was just wide open.

00:33:58.111 --> 00:34:00.285
I realized right away,

00:34:00.286 --> 00:34:03.910
that my economic tools were
not up to the task actually.

00:34:03.911 --> 00:34:07.845
This was harder and broader
and more complicated.

00:34:07.846 --> 00:34:09.265
But at the same time,

00:34:09.266 --> 00:34:11.360
I had the instinct that there

00:34:11.361 --> 00:34:14.145
really was economics here
and there was value to add.

00:34:14.146 --> 00:34:16.045
So, I felt like from
the business perspective,

00:34:16.046 --> 00:34:18.905
there were all these amazingly
brilliant engineers,

00:34:18.906 --> 00:34:21.515
but they didn't know
about advertisers,

00:34:21.516 --> 00:34:24.660
or prices, or auctions,
or things like that.

00:34:24.661 --> 00:34:26.640
But also, I learned
that they weren't as

00:34:26.641 --> 00:34:28.425
familiar with causality and

00:34:28.426 --> 00:34:30.110
that was also very important for

00:34:30.111 --> 00:34:30.870
thinking about what would

00:34:30.871 --> 00:34:32.100
happen if we changed our auction?

00:34:32.101 --> 00:34:33.815
What would happen if
the prices changed?

00:34:33.816 --> 00:34:36.200
What would happen if we
introduced this new product?

00:34:36.201 --> 00:34:37.770
So, there was a lot of

00:34:37.771 --> 00:34:39.710
contribution from
the data side as well.

00:34:39.711 --> 00:34:41.320
So, I think what, from

00:34:41.321 --> 00:34:43.510
the advice perspective,
I would say that,

00:34:43.511 --> 00:34:45.475
I think what I correctly saw,

00:34:45.476 --> 00:34:47.955
was it here was something
that was wide open.

00:34:47.956 --> 00:34:49.390
It was Greenfield.

00:34:49.391 --> 00:34:52.040
The conceptual fame.

00:34:52.041 --> 00:34:54.010
Yes I was building
on the shoulders of

00:34:54.011 --> 00:34:56.155
giants at some level,

00:34:56.156 --> 00:35:00.265
but I could actually help
lay some foundations.

00:35:00.266 --> 00:35:02.895
But I also, in some sense
had to take a big risk,

00:35:02.896 --> 00:35:04.310
because for a while first of all,

00:35:04.311 --> 00:35:05.640
I was just very busy trying to

00:35:05.641 --> 00:35:07.380
do this business work
with Microsoft,

00:35:07.381 --> 00:35:08.730
and build stuff, and learn stuff,

00:35:08.731 --> 00:35:11.790
and I didn't know SQL
and I had coded in

00:35:11.791 --> 00:35:12.800
C but not in

00:35:12.801 --> 00:35:13.900
all these other languages

00:35:13.901 --> 00:35:15.065
that were being
used in production.

00:35:15.066 --> 00:35:16.810
&gt;&gt; But you had good
experiences as

00:35:16.811 --> 00:35:19.810
an administration person
for a Unix system.

00:35:19.811 --> 00:35:23.150
&gt;&gt; I had a CS degree.
But when I did it,

00:35:23.151 --> 00:35:25.400
we learn PROLOG and LISP
and all these other stuff,

00:35:25.401 --> 00:35:27.640
so it wasn't
very relevant CS degree.

00:35:27.641 --> 00:35:28.840
But I had to and I

00:35:28.841 --> 00:35:30.265
didn't know anything
about machine learning.

00:35:30.266 --> 00:35:31.690
I didn't know the jargon there.

00:35:31.691 --> 00:35:33.150
So, I had to learn

00:35:33.151 --> 00:35:35.310
until there was a bit
of a lull in my CV,

00:35:35.311 --> 00:35:38.085
because I was working

00:35:38.086 --> 00:35:39.820
building stuff and because I had

00:35:39.821 --> 00:35:41.570
to learn all these
new disciplines.

00:35:41.571 --> 00:35:43.495
But I think that was,

00:35:43.496 --> 00:35:45.410
and then also a lot
of the economists

00:35:45.411 --> 00:35:46.520
were telling me I was crazy.

00:35:46.521 --> 00:35:48.400
This big data stuff.
That's just description.

00:35:48.401 --> 00:35:49.870
That's not economics.

00:35:49.871 --> 00:35:53.255
Machine learning that's just
fitting, that's not for us.

00:35:53.256 --> 00:35:54.925
So, there was a lot of

00:35:54.926 --> 00:35:57.170
pretty aggressive
negative feedback

00:35:57.171 --> 00:35:59.445
from my colleagues as well.

00:35:59.446 --> 00:36:01.850
But I saw this and I was just so

00:36:01.851 --> 00:36:04.090
sure that there was
something there,

00:36:04.091 --> 00:36:06.580
and I was also sure that

00:36:06.581 --> 00:36:09.165
it makes a big difference
to be a leader,

00:36:09.166 --> 00:36:11.220
if you get there first
and you're smart,

00:36:11.221 --> 00:36:14.270
and you're humble and
learn, you can be a leader.

00:36:14.271 --> 00:36:16.000
It was very likely,
I could be a leader

00:36:16.001 --> 00:36:18.875
here in a way that I
hadn't been before.

00:36:18.876 --> 00:36:21.840
There's a difference
between getting tenure,

00:36:21.841 --> 00:36:23.485
and getting prizes, and actually

00:36:23.486 --> 00:36:26.050
really being a leader
and defining a field.

00:36:26.051 --> 00:36:29.020
So, this was
my chance to do that.

00:36:29.021 --> 00:36:31.285
I took the chance
and it paid off,

00:36:31.286 --> 00:36:32.845
and I'm excited about that.

00:36:32.846 --> 00:36:34.965
But it also, it did
require being humble,

00:36:34.966 --> 00:36:36.890
going out to a computer science
conference and be like,

00:36:36.891 --> 00:36:39.715
"Who the hell are you?"

00:36:39.716 --> 00:36:40.845
There was all the stuff
I didn't know.

00:36:40.846 --> 00:36:42.180
There's a lot of stuff
I still don't know.

00:36:42.181 --> 00:36:45.685
So, it required taking
a lot of risks.

00:36:45.686 --> 00:36:47.580
But I brought the things that

00:36:47.581 --> 00:36:49.920
there's these two takeaways.

00:36:49.921 --> 00:36:52.615
One is, find something
that's young,

00:36:52.616 --> 00:36:55.555
where you can make a difference.

00:36:55.556 --> 00:36:57.370
Then the second thing
though is to be

00:36:57.371 --> 00:36:58.940
a little bit differentiated.

00:36:58.941 --> 00:37:02.550
Because in some sense, if I
was just one in a sea of,

00:37:02.551 --> 00:37:03.860
If I just completely

00:37:03.861 --> 00:37:05.775
tried to be a machine
learning person,

00:37:05.776 --> 00:37:08.295
why would I be the best one.

00:37:08.296 --> 00:37:10.695
But I found a niche,

00:37:10.696 --> 00:37:13.055
where I knew I had something
to bring to the table.

00:37:13.056 --> 00:37:14.485
Special expertise.

00:37:14.486 --> 00:37:18.520
Then, there's more of an ability
to make a contribution.

00:37:18.521 --> 00:37:20.540
So, maybe for
the young people today,

00:37:20.541 --> 00:37:22.450
there's a little sense of,

00:37:22.451 --> 00:37:24.270
there are somethings
that are getting maybe a

00:37:24.271 --> 00:37:26.605
little overdone, in AI.

00:37:26.606 --> 00:37:28.630
So, you want to be
a little bit ahead of

00:37:28.631 --> 00:37:30.775
the game rather than
behind the game.

00:37:30.776 --> 00:37:32.835
I would say, at some level

00:37:32.836 --> 00:37:34.600
this pure prediction
problem is just

00:37:34.601 --> 00:37:36.540
going to be commoditized
to some extent.

00:37:36.541 --> 00:37:39.630
We're going to get
self-tuning neural nets

00:37:39.631 --> 00:37:41.880
and AI's will tune
the neural nets.

00:37:41.881 --> 00:37:44.030
&gt;&gt; Giving all of our secrets now.

00:37:44.760 --> 00:37:48.810
&gt;&gt; That part will be
more commoditized.

00:37:48.811 --> 00:37:51.560
But then, there's still
a conceptual part

00:37:51.561 --> 00:37:54.965
of really understanding how
it works, and why it works.

00:37:54.966 --> 00:37:56.290
Which was foundational to

00:37:56.291 --> 00:37:58.240
figuring out what
the next breakthrough is.

00:37:58.241 --> 00:38:01.185
So, I guess I would suggest that

00:38:01.186 --> 00:38:03.205
the tide is going to turn back

00:38:03.206 --> 00:38:05.265
to a little bit
more conceptual insight,

00:38:05.266 --> 00:38:08.045
and if we really want to
get to human-like learning,

00:38:08.046 --> 00:38:09.980
there actually is a sense in

00:38:09.981 --> 00:38:13.145
which just fitting is
going to hit a wall.

00:38:13.146 --> 00:38:16.270
So, we're going to
have to bring back,

00:38:16.271 --> 00:38:19.170
combine that with some modeling.

00:38:19.171 --> 00:38:20.980
Because it's just
a fact that you can't

00:38:20.981 --> 00:38:23.155
extrapolate without a model.

00:38:23.156 --> 00:38:28.180
&gt;&gt; What do you think
some big feasible surprises

00:38:28.181 --> 00:38:31.610
in the field of economics will
be over the next 15 years?

00:38:31.611 --> 00:38:33.700
What things might happen even if

00:38:33.701 --> 00:38:34.860
it describe in
a little bit more of

00:38:34.861 --> 00:38:36.345
abstractly that would be,

00:38:36.346 --> 00:38:38.840
almost surprising from
today's point of view,

00:38:38.841 --> 00:38:43.750
that will rely heavily on
economic inferences and models,

00:38:43.751 --> 00:38:45.350
and their use in the world.

00:38:45.351 --> 00:38:47.605
&gt;&gt; Well, I think at some level

00:38:47.606 --> 00:38:50.070
there's different parts
of economics.

00:38:50.071 --> 00:38:51.960
So, I think in some level,

00:38:51.961 --> 00:38:55.595
the economic ideas
may not change.

00:38:55.596 --> 00:38:58.265
If I see something where
the data is going to come in,

00:38:58.266 --> 00:39:01.705
it's going to be the personalization
changes or insights.

00:39:01.706 --> 00:39:05.320
There's a whole bunch of
some findings right now.

00:39:05.321 --> 00:39:07.240
That stuff doesn't work.

00:39:07.241 --> 00:39:10.220
We go out and evaluate
training programs,

00:39:10.221 --> 00:39:12.520
and they do a little bit but
they don't work that much.

00:39:12.521 --> 00:39:15.280
But, the typical of
economic measurement

00:39:15.281 --> 00:39:16.670
of a government project

00:39:16.671 --> 00:39:18.680
or something like that
is that it doesn't work.

00:39:18.681 --> 00:39:20.670
But a lot of these things.

00:39:20.671 --> 00:39:22.025
&gt;&gt; The actual model
doesn't work or the

00:39:22.026 --> 00:39:23.855
government program doesn't work?

00:39:23.856 --> 00:39:25.165
&gt;&gt; The program doesn't
work. So, there's a lot of-

00:39:25.166 --> 00:39:25.880
&gt;&gt; Or both.

00:39:25.881 --> 00:39:28.180
&gt;&gt; There's a lot of negativity.

00:39:28.181 --> 00:39:32.350
Things like educational
interventions don't work,

00:39:32.351 --> 00:39:34.600
health interventions don't work.

00:39:34.601 --> 00:39:39.300
But I think that what we've
learned from tech companies,

00:39:39.301 --> 00:39:41.410
is that you shouldn't think

00:39:41.411 --> 00:39:44.284
about coming up with
one grand idea,

00:39:44.285 --> 00:39:47.260
and then go out and spend
a year testing that one idea.

00:39:47.261 --> 00:39:50.175
But rather you
iteratively improve.

00:39:50.176 --> 00:39:51.105
&gt;&gt; Run flights.

00:39:51.106 --> 00:39:52.550
&gt;&gt; Run flights but lots of them

00:39:52.551 --> 00:39:54.140
and you incrementally improve.

00:39:54.141 --> 00:39:56.650
So, I believe, that

00:39:56.651 --> 00:39:57.770
the economist is going to

00:39:57.771 --> 00:39:59.840
become much more of an engineer,

00:39:59.841 --> 00:40:02.000
because the some of

00:40:02.001 --> 00:40:07.485
these economic problems like
retraining the workforce,

00:40:07.486 --> 00:40:11.185
finding government
programs that work,

00:40:11.186 --> 00:40:15.000
are going to be recast
as digital programs.

00:40:15.001 --> 00:40:18.330
Digital programs are amenable
to incremental innovation.

00:40:18.331 --> 00:40:22.435
So, we will join up
with the engineers,

00:40:22.436 --> 00:40:25.570
and come up with methods
to incrementally improve.

00:40:25.571 --> 00:40:26.850
So, where that meets

00:40:26.851 --> 00:40:28.710
the current machine
learning literature

00:40:28.711 --> 00:40:31.265
in bandit's, contextual bandit's,

00:40:31.266 --> 00:40:35.275
reinforcement learning,
and that side of AI,

00:40:35.276 --> 00:40:37.030
which has always
actually had a bit of

00:40:37.031 --> 00:40:39.005
a causal bent to it.

00:40:39.006 --> 00:40:41.980
Because you're trying to
understand what works.

00:40:41.981 --> 00:40:43.060
What treatment to give.

00:40:43.061 --> 00:40:44.370
So, that's going
to come together.

00:40:44.371 --> 00:40:45.710
So, my prediction is that

00:40:45.711 --> 00:40:48.320
economists are going to
become more engineers,

00:40:48.321 --> 00:40:51.815
and instead of complaining
that nothing works,

00:40:51.816 --> 00:40:53.845
we're going to start
building things

00:40:53.846 --> 00:40:57.030
that do work to achieve
economic outcomes,

00:40:57.031 --> 00:40:58.595
to make governments
more efficient.

00:40:58.596 --> 00:41:00.790
But economists ranges
on resource allocation.

00:41:00.791 --> 00:41:02.070
So, we're going to do that on

00:41:02.071 --> 00:41:05.000
resource allocation problems,
pricing problems.

00:41:05.001 --> 00:41:08.070
So, we're going to both
figure out what works,

00:41:08.071 --> 00:41:09.500
we're going to
realize that nothing

00:41:09.501 --> 00:41:11.365
works if it's one-size-fits-all.

00:41:11.366 --> 00:41:13.000
But there are a lot
of things work if

00:41:13.001 --> 00:41:14.720
they're actually personalized and

00:41:14.721 --> 00:41:18.750
appropriately delivered with
the correct information.

00:41:18.751 --> 00:41:21.870
&gt;&gt; Even imagine someday a
congressional hearing about how

00:41:21.871 --> 00:41:25.640
much to explore versus exploit
in a program be fabulous.

00:41:25.641 --> 00:41:28.780
&gt;&gt; Well, I think that's
actually not so crazy.

00:41:28.781 --> 00:41:30.290
When I teach to

00:41:30.291 --> 00:41:31.770
business leaders and boards

00:41:31.771 --> 00:41:33.510
of directors and talk about AI,

00:41:33.511 --> 00:41:35.920
and actually what they
need to do at some level

00:41:35.921 --> 00:41:37.260
the high-level leaders
of a firm to

00:41:37.261 --> 00:41:39.190
figure out how to
regulate the AI.

00:41:39.191 --> 00:41:40.515
They need to figure out

00:41:40.516 --> 00:41:42.590
what guidelines
should I be setting.

00:41:42.591 --> 00:41:45.255
What makes it safe. How
do I make sure it's good.

00:41:45.256 --> 00:41:48.370
Governments have the same role,

00:41:48.371 --> 00:41:50.495
the banking regulators
have to figure out

00:41:50.496 --> 00:41:53.080
how do the AI that
are used in banks,

00:41:53.081 --> 00:41:55.110
what makes it safe,
what makes it fair.

00:41:55.111 --> 00:41:59.675
So, this broader view
of regulating

00:41:59.676 --> 00:42:05.095
and setting the objectives
for AI, is hugely impactful.

00:42:05.096 --> 00:42:06.660
So, that's partly why
you might have asked

00:42:06.661 --> 00:42:08.830
in my previous answer,

00:42:08.831 --> 00:42:11.690
why would economists have
anything to do with this.

00:42:11.691 --> 00:42:15.775
But actually when the AI is
solving an economic problem,

00:42:15.776 --> 00:42:18.210
and it's living in
a real world setting,

00:42:18.211 --> 00:42:19.785
what it optimizes?

00:42:19.786 --> 00:42:20.955
What are the measures?

00:42:20.956 --> 00:42:22.545
How do you judge success.

00:42:22.546 --> 00:42:24.350
What constraints
do you put on it?

00:42:24.351 --> 00:42:26.095
Is it a decision support tool?

00:42:26.096 --> 00:42:27.875
Or does it actually
make the decision.

00:42:27.876 --> 00:42:29.350
All of those.

00:42:29.351 --> 00:42:30.240
&gt;&gt; Whose values.

00:42:30.241 --> 00:42:32.065
&gt;&gt; Whose values are represented.

00:42:32.066 --> 00:42:33.930
All of this stuff,

00:42:33.931 --> 00:42:38.195
really becomes- you don't
decompose the problems,

00:42:38.196 --> 00:42:40.755
it becomes an integrated problem

00:42:40.756 --> 00:42:42.520
and that's why I feel like

00:42:42.521 --> 00:42:45.640
these interdisciplinary
teams will

00:42:45.641 --> 00:42:48.630
work together to create this,

00:42:48.631 --> 00:42:49.730
but I feel like we're
actually going to

00:42:49.731 --> 00:42:51.220
do a lot of good in the world.

00:42:51.221 --> 00:42:53.300
&gt;&gt; On that note. Thanks
very much, Susan.

00:42:53.301 --> 00:42:55.010
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

